+    // WebAssembly shadow stack: 1 MiB by default there, against 8 MiB on a
+    // Linux main thread.
+    //
+    // `MAX_CALL_DEPTH` is 1000 nested Redblue calls, and the interpreter
+    // natively gets the room for them from `STACK_BYTES_PER_CALL` in a thread
+    // it spawns per run. `wasm32-unknown-unknown` has no threads, so the same
+    // depth has to fit in the one stack — and at the default it does not: a
+    // Redblue function that called another trapped with "memory access out of
+    // bounds", which killed the whole module instance rather than returning an
+    // error.
+    //
+    // This is a *link* argument, not a limit the interpreter reads and not a
+    // bound a program can reach: it gives the playground the room
+    // `run_isolated` gives it natively, so the two builds accept and refuse the
+    // same programs. It is emitted here rather than in `.cargo/config.toml`
+    // because an environment `RUSTFLAGS` — which CI sets — takes precedence
+    // over `target.*.rustflags` and would silently discard it. Non-wasm targets
+    // are untouched.
+    //
+    // The target is read from `CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ARCH` rather than a `#[cfg]`
+    // because a build script is compiled for the *host*, so `cfg!(wasm32)`
+    // here is false on a Linux machine cross-compiling to wasm — which is
+    // exactly the case that needs the flag. The `#[cfg]` above is fine for the
+    // Windows arm only because that one is always a native build.
+    if std::env::var("CARGO_CFG_TARGET_ARCH").as_deref() == Ok("wasm32") {
